2186179781 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281421 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179781 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281423 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179781 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281424 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179781 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281425 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179781 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281426 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179781 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389256 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179781 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389263 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179781 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186406999 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179781 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the Types of Images Formed by an Affine Transformation" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179781 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179442 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179781 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4e551b65-4116-58bc-b2bc-e536930c69b4" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179866 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180565 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179866 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180566 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179866 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180567 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179866 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180568 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179866 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180569 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179866 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180574 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179866 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180575 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179866 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180576 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179866 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179866 #edb/ref 1045 "The Inverse of an Affine Transformation" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179866 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186180565 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179866 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048498 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179866 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "1c5ce268-312c-52a6-9777-1ff390251f96" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180095 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280894 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180095 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162151" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180095 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180095 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3f/3f8c1c9854ec54dd3caf4487328d3f3c35f2d69680a8fd1c13d28125dac77d03.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nWhat would be the image of the square shown above under the action of $T?$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180095 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "79161a5e-44bb-5845-8a74-564fa863b598" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180095 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-3)-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible and the image of the square under the action of $T$ is a line segment." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180565 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180566 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180565 #edb/ref 1061 44660 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180565 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "35dfb0c0-efab-5193-92eb-6eb22cc2af9f" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180565 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180763 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180566 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180567 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180566 #edb/ref 1061 44661 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180566 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "30275202-8cd8-51e0-a9e1-92aa287dbe42" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180566 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179776 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180567 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180568 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180567 #edb/ref 1061 44664 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180567 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6802d261-21db-5122-886a-1dda8f3a0db4" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180567 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180764 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180568 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180569 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180568 #edb/ref 1061 44665 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180568 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3f8aecda-4d54-5528-939f-886f4ab93593" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180568 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179777 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180569 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180575 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180569 #edb/ref 1061 44668 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180569 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "21d1cffb-5a12-5ee7-ad2a-181e2a8d4474" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180569 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179778 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180574 #edb/ref 1061 44704 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180574 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "35abe35f-9e29-539b-aaa5-a2546fd2fbfb" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180574 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179781 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180575 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180576 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180575 #edb/ref 1061 44705 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180575 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5ec4662b-6870-532f-83f9-f7a9cbe5504e" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180575 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180767 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180576 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180574 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180576 #edb/ref 1061 44723 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180576 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d71cf118-7383-5aea-a47c-8359fc7dffe4" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180576 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180768 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180763 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is an affine transformation. The **inverse** of $\\mathbf T$ (if it exists) is an affine transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ such that\n\n$$\\mathbf T^{-1} (\\mathbf T(\\mathbf u)) = \\mathbf T (\\mathbf T^{-1}(\\mathbf u)) = \\mathbf u$$\n\nfor every vector $\\mathbf u\\in\\mathbb R^2.$ In other words, the transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ reverses the action of $\\mathbf T.$\n\nNot all affine transformations are invertible. So how can we determine whether a given affine transformation is invertible?\n\nTo answer this, recall that an affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ can be expressed in matrix form as\n\n$$\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. We can reverse the action of $\\mathbf T$ by making $\\mathbf u$ the subject of this equation, as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ {A}^{-1}\\cdot Au & ={A}^{-1}\\cdot (x-b) \\\\ {I}_{2}\\cdot u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b) \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b)\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThis equation gives us a method of computing the inverse of an affine transformation. Additionally, we have the following:\n\n*An affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ is invertible if and only if the matrix $A$ that represents the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T$ is invertible, that is, $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180763 #edb/ref 1065 7697 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180763 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bf99caa7-1e47-5fea-87ac-9b91151af127" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180763 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180764 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T,$ expressed in matrix form as\n\n$$\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{\\mathbf x} = \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{A} \\cdot \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{\\mathbf u} + \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{\\mathbf b}.$$\n\nWe wish to determine whether $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ exists and find its matrix form if it does exist.\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get $$\\det(A) = \\left| \\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned} \\right| = 1\\cdot (-2) - (-3) \\cdot 1 = 1 \\neq 0.$$\n\nSince $\\det(A) \\neq 0,$ the affine transformation is invertible.\n\nTo compute the inverse of $\\mathbf T,$ we will use the formula we derived earlier:\n\n$$\\mathbf{u} = A^{-1}(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b})$$\n\nWe start by calculating the inverse of $A{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix form of the inverse affine transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180764 #edb/ref 1065 7698 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180764 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c4c88a3e-6514-5b56-ab23-306164b0a3a3" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180764 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the Inverse of an Affine Transformation" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180767 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the square in the $uv$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/a8/a8cc0923da86a1a1ac6b666893397263ef3865448080500caa6f17510547d901.png)\n\nWe wish to explore the image of this square under an affine transformation $$\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbf{u} \\longrightarrow A \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{b},$$ where $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, and $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors.\n\nSimilar to linear transformations, there are three possibilities depending on the value of $\\det(A)$.\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular ($\\det(A)\\neq 0$). In this case, our square is mapped to a parallelogram.\n\nFor example, let the transformation $\\mathbf T$ be given by $$[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] = [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] + [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$$ Mapping our square to the $xy$-plane, we get the following parallelogram:\n\n![](images/36/36da067393fa53f4296373d65e3c6177a54d1a901824188687b4a74ea171afdf.png)\n- $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular ($\\det(A)=0$) and $A$ has at least one nonzero entry. In this case, our square is mapped to a line segment.\n\nFor example, let the transformation $\\mathbf T$ be given by $$[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] = [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] + [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$$ Mapping our square to the $xy$-plane gives the following line segment:\n\n![](images/5e/5e5481e5b2f2218a6097020e260dc991cde05574372c724aa07527458257d94c.png)\n- $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular ($\\det(A)=0$) and $A$ is the zero matrix. In this case, our square is mapped to a point on the plane.\n\nFor example, let the transformation $\\mathbf T$ be given by $$[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] = [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] + [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$$ Mapping our square to the $xy$-plane gives the following point.\n\n![](images/00/0072c5e06674f79f82e771744fda217e6558830f7a6e4ab387aa9dbf4dda09bc.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180767 #edb/ref 1065 7706 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180767 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "375d6591-08dd-5dbf-b36a-28013839a28b" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180767 #edb/ref 1067 "Image Types Formed by Affine Transformations" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180768 #edb/ref 1064 "In general, if we consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\Bbb R^2 \\to \\Bbb R^2,$ defined by the mapping\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbf{u} \\longrightarrow A \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{b},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, then we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) \\neq 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) = 0,$ we have two additional subcases: \n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane (namely, the point with position vector $\\mathbf b$)." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180768 #edb/ref 1065 7710 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180768 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "560907bc-354a-5693-a8df-e6824454755d" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180768 #edb/ref 1067 "A Summary of Image Types" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186256902 #edb/ref 1081 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186256902 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186256902 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "0bed1340-8927-5ff0-ba69-b2b93cdd78e8" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186257407 #edb/ref 1081 "-3" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186257407 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186257407 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "46c52d4e-2631-53e7-8a00-72ab68871a97" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186257807 #edb/ref 1081 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186257807 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186257807 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "746b1cdb-7c35-5efe-a92c-2cd06400438a" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186257943 #edb/ref 1081 "-2" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186257943 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186257943 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "81fb632e-14da-5038-b6f9-74ec0da963a5" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186257947 #edb/ref 1081 "-4" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186257947 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186257947 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "82d7f633-617d-5497-a90c-a0550510c2f6" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186258217 #edb/ref 1081 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186258217 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186258217 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "a34beb49-2bb7-5107-84ce-cae801ce1c47" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186258655 #edb/ref 1081 "-6" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186258655 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186258655 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "d5507f11-f2dc-54ed-aa00-8c211067e5e5" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186258766 #edb/ref 1081 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186258766 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186258766 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "e3817db8-3192-5a2c-ad51-eb7cb8e7b55c" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186258863 #edb/ref 1081 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186258863 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186258863 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "ef2b02b6-f203-59a0-ab8f-6e907c4aac2b" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186258956 #edb/ref 1081 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186258956 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186258956 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "f75572fb-3d5c-57c1-b45a-ea11a4c996bd" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259309 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186257407 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259309 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186257807 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259309 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186258217 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259309 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186258863 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259309 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186258956 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259309 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259309 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259309 #edb/ref 1079 #edb/ref 17592186257807 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259309 #edb/ref 1080 #uuid "99e69744-50ea-5409-9359-8197e1464b98" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259479 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186256902 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259479 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186257943 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259479 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186257947 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259479 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186258655 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259479 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186258766 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259479 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259479 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259479 #edb/ref 1079 #edb/ref 17592186258655 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259479 #edb/ref 1080 #uuid "fa29d767-9911-5a05-a828-3b3c81faca3c" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259639 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259309 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259639 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124241" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259639 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259639 #edb/ref 1071 "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 9 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ -1 & c\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259639 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "08a12b2e-6f73-5fc0-987c-f4d58e5bc21f" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259639 #edb/ref 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 9 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 8-9\\cdot 4}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -9 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -9 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=\\frac{5}{4}+(-\\frac{9}{4})+2=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259655 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259479 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259655 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124280" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259655 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259655 #edb/ref 1071 "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=2-u-v,\\,y=3u+4v-1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=7+ax-y,\\,v=bx+y+c,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259655 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f20eb077-8cae-5138-ae0e-248f60ff9fb8" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186259655 #edb/ref 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2-u-v \\\\ 3u+4v-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-u-v \\\\ 3u+4v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ y+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4\\cdot (x-2)+(-1)\\cdot (y+1) \\\\ 3\\cdot (x-2)+1\\cdot (y+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7-4x-y \\\\ 3x+y-5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=7+(-4)x-y,\\,v=3x+y+(-5).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-4+3+(-5)=-6.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186278842 #edb/ref 1081 "A parallelepiped" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186278842 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186278842 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "1acc367e-71f3-5e97-87ce-92842c5b3257" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186279159 #edb/ref 1081 "A parallelogram" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186279159 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186279159 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "3f4b22b4-65fa-5db1-94f1-780f0591f6e8" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186279503 #e